#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721655 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/fc/fc8694766f760121178e27fdd53d5a05024661addbed5e41e22cd28a3e4bd7b9.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $-2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}$.\n\n$-2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨3,3⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨-1,-4⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b} &=(-2)\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩+(-3)\\cdot ⟨-1,-4⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-6,-6⟩+⟨3,12⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-6+3,-6+12⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721660 :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C x^2y^3\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the lower-left quarter of the ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,-2)$, as shown above.\n" :question/worked-solution "Write the ellipse as $x^2+y^2/4=1$. A parameterization that follows the indicated quarter-ellipse is\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\nAt these bounds the points are $(-1,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively. Use the signed derivative $y'(t)=2\\cos t$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C x^2y^3\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_\\pi^{3\\pi/2}(\\cos^2 t)(2\\sin t)^3(2\\cos t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=16\\int_\\pi^{3\\pi/2}\\sin^3 t\\cos^3 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo integrate, keep one factor of $\\cos t$ for the differential and use $\\cos^2 t=1-\\sin^2 t$. With $u=\\sin t$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, the bounds become $0$ and $-1$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16\\int_\\pi^{3\\pi/2}\\sin^3 t\\cos^2 t\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n&=16\\int_0^{-1}u^3(1-u^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=16\\left[\\frac{u^4}{4}-\\frac{u^6}{6}\\right]_0^{-1}\\\\\n&=16\\left(\\frac14-\\frac16\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac43.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe positive result is consistent with the direction: $x^2y^3$ is negative inside this arc and $y$ decreases, so the signed contributions are positive.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721667 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(2,1)$, directed as shown above. Use the parameterization\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1+t,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1,\n$$\nand the formula\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "Here $x(t)=1+t$ and $y(t)=t$, so $x'(t)=1$ and $f(\\mathbf r(t))=1+2t$. The bounds follow the indicated direction: $\\mathbf r(0)=(1,0)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(2,1)$.\n\nSubstitute into the supplied formula and evaluate:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_0^1(1+2t)\\cdot1\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[t+t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, so no arc-length factor is needed.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721677 :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C (y^2-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3)$, directed as shown above.\n" :question/worked-solution "Parameterize the directed segment by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t,1+2t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\nThis gives the stated initial and terminal points. Because the integral is with respect to $y$, use $y'(t)=2$:\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\nSubstituting both coordinates gives\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(1+2t)^2-2t=1+2t+4t^2.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(y^2-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=2\\int_0^1(1+2t+4t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=2\\left[t+t^2+\\frac{4t^3}{3}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2\\left(1+1+\\frac43\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{20}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721696 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=x^{2} + 1$ from $A(1,2)$ to $B(2,5)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\qquad y(t)=t^{2} + 1.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\qquad y'(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(2 \\left(t^{2} + 1\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=2 t^{4} + 4 t^{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(2 t^{4} + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 t^{5}}{5} + 2 t\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{84}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{72}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721701 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=\\sqrt{x}$ from $A(1,1)$ to $B(4,2)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x^{2} - y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^{2},\\qquad y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 t,\\qquad y'(t)=1.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(\\left(t^{2}\\right)^{2} - \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=2 t^{5} - 2 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t^{2}\\right) + 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(2 t^{5} - 4 t^{2} + 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{t^{6}}{3} - \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{44}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{44}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721712 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=x^{2}$ from $A(1,1)$ to $B(2,4)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\qquad y(t)=t^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\qquad y'(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(- \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- \\frac{t^{4}}{2} - t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(-12\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{21}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721716 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=2 \\sqrt{x}$ from $A(1,2)$ to $B(4,4)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^{2},\\qquad y(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 t,\\qquad y'(t)=2.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t^{2}\\right) + 2 \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t^{3} + 8 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(\\left(t^{2}\\right) + \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=2 t^{2} + 4 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 4 t^{3} + 10 t^{2} + 4 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- t^{4} + \\frac{10 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{56}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{43}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721719 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ from $A(0,0)$ to $B(1,1)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(4 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^{3},\\qquad y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=0$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3 t^{2},\\qquad y'(t)=1.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(4 \\left(t\\right) e^{\\left(t^{3}\\right) \\left(t\\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(4 t e^{t^{4}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=12 t^{3} e^{t^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 t^{3} e^{t^{4}} - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- t + 3 e^{t^{4}}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\left(-1 + 3 e\\right)-\\left(3\\right) \\\\ &=-4 + 3 e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721734 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 x - y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $4 x^{2} + y^{2}=4$, traversed clockwise from $\\left(1,0\\right)$ to $\\left(-1,0\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and clockwise traversal give $t$ from $2 \\pi$ to $\\pi$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{2 \\pi}^{\\pi}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{2 \\pi}^{\\pi}\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=4 t - \\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[4 t - \\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{2 \\pi}^{\\pi} \\\\ &= \\left(4 \\pi\\right)-\\left(8 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &= - 4 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721748 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$, traversed clockwise from $\\left(0,-1\\right)$ to $\\left(0,1\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and clockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ to $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=- 3 t - 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[- 3 t - 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\left(-8 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{9 \\pi}{2} - 8\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721751 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=36$, traversed counterclockwise from $\\left(0,-3\\right)$ to $\\left(2,0\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and counterclockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ to $2 \\pi$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- 22 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=- 6 t - 11 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[- 6 t - 11 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &= \\left(- 12 \\pi\\right)-\\left(- 9 \\pi - 11\\right) \\\\ &= 11 - 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721754 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + y^{2}=9$, traversed counterclockwise from $\\left(0,-3\\right)$ to $\\left(0,3\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and counterclockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ to $\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}}\\left(20 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=- 6 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[- 6 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\left(10 - 15 \\pi\\right)-\\left(10 - 9 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &= - 6 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721761 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 x - y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + y^{2}=9$, traversed counterclockwise from $\\left(0,3\\right)$ to $\\left(0,-3\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and counterclockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(20 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=3 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[3 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\left(10 + \\frac{9 \\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2} + 10\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721772 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,2)$ to $B(0,4)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,2\\rangle+t\\langle -2,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 - 2 t,2 t + 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 - 2 t$, $y(t)=2 t + 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -2,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 y^{2}$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - 2 t\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=8 t^{2} - 16 t + 8, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=16 t^{2} + 32 t + 16.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(24 t^{2} + 16 t + 24\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 24 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 24 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=8 \\left(1\\right)^{3} + 8 \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 24 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=40, \\\\\nF(0)&=8 \\left(0\\right)^{3} + 8 \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 24 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(40\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=40.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721778 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,-2)$ to $B(1,0)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 0,-2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t,2 t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t$, $y(t)=2 t - 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=4 t - 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 t - 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - 4 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - 4 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} - 4 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{5}{2}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} - 4 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721780 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,2)$ to $B(0,1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,2\\rangle+t\\langle -1,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 1 - t,2 - t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=1 - t$, $y(t)=2 - t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=- y^{2}$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - t\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=- t^{2} + 2 t - 1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - t\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=t^{2} - 4 t + 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 - 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- t^{2} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- t^{2} + 3 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=2, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721790 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-1,-1)$ to $B(-2,0)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle+t\\langle -1,1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle - t - 1,t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=- t - 1$, $y(t)=t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=2 x$ and $Q(x,y)=- y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t + 2, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=1 - t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(t + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + 3 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{2}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} + 3 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721792 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-1,-2)$ to $B(0,-3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t - 1,- t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t - 1$, $y(t)=- t - 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- y$ and $Q(x,y)=- 2 x$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=t + 2, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t - 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721804 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-3,1)$ to $B(-4,3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle - t - 2,2 t - 1\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(1)&=\\langle -3,1\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(2)&=\\langle -4,3\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $2$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=- t - 2$, $y(t)=2 t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x y$ and $Q(x,y)=1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2} - 3 t + 2, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 2 t^{2} - 3 t + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} + 4 t\\right]_{1}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} + 4 t, \\\\\nF(2)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2} + 4 \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{10}{3}, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 4 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(2)-F(1) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{10}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{11}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{31}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721808 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(4,4)$ to $B(0,2)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle 2 t,t + 2\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(2)&=\\langle 4,4\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(0)&=\\langle 0,2\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $2$ to $0$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t$, $y(t)=t + 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} - 8 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{2}^{0}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{2}^{0}\\left(- 4 t^{2} - 8 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - 4 t^{2} - t\\right]_{2}^{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - 4 t^{2} - t, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - 4 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0, \\\\\nF(2)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(2\\right)^{3}}{3} - 4 \\left(2\\right)^{2} - \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{86}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(0)-F(2) \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{86}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{86}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721825 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,-4)$ to $B(2,-6)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle t,- 2 t - 2\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(1)&=\\langle 1,-4\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(2)&=\\langle 2,-6\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $2$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=t$, $y(t)=- 2 t - 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2} - 2 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 2 t^{2} - 2 t + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t\\right]_{1}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t, \\\\\nF(2)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{16}{3}, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(2)-F(1) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{17}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721826 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-2,-1)$ to $B(2,3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle - 2 t,1 - 2 t\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(1)&=\\langle -2,-1\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(-1)&=\\langle 2,3\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $-1$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=- 2 t$, $y(t)=1 - 2 t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -2,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- y$ and $Q(x,y)=x y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=2 - 4 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 8 t^{2} + 4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{-1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{-1}\\left(2 - 8 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t\\right]_{1}^{-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t, \\\\\nF(-1)&=- \\frac{8 \\left(-1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{8 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(-1)-F(1) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721829 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,1)$ to $B(2,3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t + 1,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t + 1$, $y(t)=2 t + 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t + 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t^{2} + 3 t + 1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t^{2} + 3 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{6}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721832 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,1)$ to $B(4,0)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,1\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t + 2,1 - t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t + 2$, $y(t)=1 - t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - t\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=4 - 4 t^{2}, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(5 - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 5 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 5 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 5 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} + 5 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721834 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-1,-1)$ to $B(0,1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t - 1,2 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t - 1$, $y(t)=2 t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=- x y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} + 6 t - 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 4 t^{2} + 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 3 t^{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 3 t^{2} - t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} + 3 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721839 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,0)$ to $B(4,-1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,0\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t + 2,- t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t + 2$, $y(t)=- t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=- x$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} - 4 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t + 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 4 t^{2} - 2 t + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721858 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(3,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe angle $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ lies in the second quadrant and has reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Hence cosine is negative and sine is positive:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=3\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n &=\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$. The negative $x$ and positive $y$ confirm the second quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721862 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(4,\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe right triangle shows why cosine gives the horizontal component and sine gives the vertical component.\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nHere $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. First calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n &=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates are positive, as expected in the first quadrant. Therefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3},2\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721870 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}k,\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$, where $k$ is a positive real number. Find its exact Cartesian coordinates in terms of $k$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the same conversion formulas, keeping the radius in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $k>0$, the radius $2\\sqrt{3}k$ is positive. The angle $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ lies in the third quadrant and has reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$, so\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2\\sqrt{3}k\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}k\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-\\sqrt{3}k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=2\\sqrt{3}k\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}k\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-3k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\sqrt{3}k,-3k\\right)$. Both coordinates are negative for every allowed $k$, as expected in the third quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721875 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-2,1)$ to $B(0,-1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -2,1\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t - 2,1 - 2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t - 2$, $y(t)=1 - 2 t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x y$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=8 t^{2} - 12 t + 4, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=4 - 4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(8 t^{2} - 16 t + 8\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} - 8 t^{2} + 8 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} - 8 t^{2} + 8 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{8 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - 8 \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 8 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{8 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - 8 \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 8 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721879 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,0)$ to $B(-1,-1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 0,0\\rangle+t\\langle -1,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle - t,- t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=- t$, $y(t)=- t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=- t^{2}, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- t^{2} - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{t^{3}}{3} - t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{t^{3}}{3} - t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{4}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721895 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,1)$ to $B(-1,-1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 0,1\\rangle+t\\langle -1,-2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle - t,1 - 2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=- t$, $y(t)=1 - 2 t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x y$ and $Q(x,y)=1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t^{2} - t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t^{2} - t - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{t^{2}}{2} - 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{t^{2}}{2} - 2 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{11}{6}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} - 2 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{11}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{11}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721901 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,-1)$ to $B(2,-3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t + 1,- 2 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t + 1$, $y(t)=- 2 t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=- x y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t + 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} - 6 t - 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 4 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - 3 t^{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - 3 t^{2} - t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - 3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{16}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - 3 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{16}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721903 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $2$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $30^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with $\\cos 30^{\\circ}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin 30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nThe diagram shows how the two coordinates relate to the radius and angle.\n\n![A first-quadrant right triangle with radius r, angle theta, horizontal leg x equals r cosine theta, and vertical leg y equals r sine theta.](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)" :question/worked-solution "The description gives the polar quantities $r=2$ and $\\theta=30^{\\circ}$. The horizontal coordinate uses cosine and the vertical coordinate uses sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta=2\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3},\\\\\ny&=r\\sin\\theta=2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates are positive because $P$ is in the first quadrant. In the order $(x,y)$, the Cartesian coordinates are $(\\sqrt{3},1)$. The distance is $\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^2+1^2}=2$, as required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721914 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $2\\sqrt{3}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. The angle lies in the second quadrant with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nConvert each coordinate, including the radical in the radius:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta=(2\\sqrt{3})\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{3}=-3,\\\\\ny&=r\\sin\\theta=(2\\sqrt{3})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian coordinates are $(-3,\\sqrt{3})$. The signs agree with the second quadrant, and $x^2+y^2=9+3=12=(2\\sqrt{3})^2$. Multiplying the two square roots in the $x$-coordinate gives $3$, rather than leaving a single factor of $\\sqrt{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721918 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $210^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. What are the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=210^{\\circ}$. This angle is in the third quadrant with reference angle $30^{\\circ}$, so both cosine and sine are negative:\n\n$$\n\\cos 210^{\\circ}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin 210^{\\circ}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos 210^{\\circ}=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\\ny&=3\\sin 210^{\\circ}=3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{27}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}=9=r^2$, and both coordinates have the third-quadrant signs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721924 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$, where $r>0$, and Cartesian coordinates $(2,0)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nUse $x=R\\cos\\phi$ and $y=R\\sin\\phi$ for a point with polar coordinates $(R,\\phi)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $A=(2,0)$ lies on the positive $x$-axis, its distance from the origin is $r=2$. Thus the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2r,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=(4,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe lesson's right triangle shows why the Cartesian coordinates of a point are its radius times the cosine and sine of its angle:\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nUsing the radius $4$ and angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ of $B$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=2,\\\\\ny_B&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2,2\\sqrt{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721937 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$." :question/worked-solution "First, find the reference angle of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\alpha=\\arctan\\left(\\left|\\frac{y_A}{x_A}\\right|\\right)=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nSince $x_A<0$ and $y_A>0$, the point $A$ lies in the second quadrant. We may take\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\pi-\\alpha=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe angle of $B$ is therefore\n\n$$\n\\phi_B=\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{3}=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}+\\frac{2\\pi}{6}=\\frac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nAny coterminal choice of $\\theta$ gives the same Cartesian point. The polar coordinates of $B$ are $(3,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Now convert using $x_B=3\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=3\\sin\\phi_B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\\ny_B&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721939 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi}{4})$, where $r>0$, and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the radius of $A$ from its Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2}\\\\\n &=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{2})^2}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. We use $x_B=R_B\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=R_B\\sin\\phi_B$ with the radius and angle of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$. Both coordinates are negative, as expected for an angle in the third quadrant."}]}
